{"id":"deuxieme-plus-grande-valeur","title":"Deuxième plus grande valeur","titleEn":"Second largest value","chapter":"p1-conditions","level":"p1","track":"course","synthesis":false,"difficulty":"facile","order":7,"tags":["conditions","boucles"],"points":50,"timeLimitMs":1000,"text":{"fr":{"title":"Deuxième plus grande valeur","statement":"<p>Lire <code>N</code> entiers et afficher la deuxième plus grande valeur, c&#39;est-à-dire celle qui arrive en deuxième position quand on range les valeurs de la plus grande à la plus petite. Si la plus grande valeur apparaît plusieurs fois, la réponse est donc cette même valeur.</p>\n","input":"<ul>\n<li>La première ligne contient l&#39;entier <code>N</code>.</li>\n<li>Les <code>N</code> lignes suivantes contiennent chacune un entier.</li>\n</ul>\n","output":"<p>Un seul entier : la deuxième plus grande valeur.</p>\n","constraints":"<ul>\n<li>2 ≤ N ≤ 1 000</li>\n<li>−1 000 000 000 ≤ chaque valeur ≤ 1 000 000 000</li>\n</ul>\n","hints":["<p>On garde deux variables : <code>premier</code>, la plus grande valeur vue jusqu&#39;ici, et <code>second</code>, la deuxième.</p>\n","<p>Pour démarrer, on lit les deux premières valeurs et on range la plus grande dans <code>premier</code>, l&#39;autre dans <code>second</code>.</p>\n","<p>Une nouvelle valeur <code>x</code> peut détrôner <code>premier</code> (et alors l&#39;ancien <code>premier</code> devient <code>second</code>), ou seulement <code>second</code>, ou rien du tout.</p>\n"],"solution":"<p>On tient à jour les deux plus grandes valeurs vues jusqu&#39;ici, avec <code>premier ≥ second</code>. Au départ, on lit deux valeurs et on les place dans le bon ordre. Puis, pour chaque nouvelle valeur <code>x</code> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>si <code>x &gt; premier</code>, l&#39;ancien premier passe deuxième : <code>second = premier</code>, puis <code>premier = x</code> ;</li>\n<li>sinon, si <code>x &gt; second</code>, <code>x</code> prend seulement la deuxième place : <code>second = x</code> ;</li>\n<li>sinon, rien ne change.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>L&#39;ordre des deux affectations du premier cas compte : si on écrit d&#39;abord <code>premier = x</code>, l&#39;ancienne valeur de <code>premier</code> est perdue.</p>\n<p>Ce raisonnement gère aussi les valeurs répétées. Avec <code>9, 4, 9</code>, le troisième <code>9</code> n&#39;est pas plus grand que <code>premier</code>, mais il est plus grand que <code>second</code> (4) : il prend la deuxième place, et la réponse est <code>9</code>, comme demandé. Il faut aussi faire attention à ne pas partir de <code>0</code> : toutes les valeurs peuvent être négatives.</p>\n"},"en":{"title":"Second largest value","statement":"<p>Read <code>N</code> integers and print the second largest value, that is, the one in second position when the values are sorted from largest to smallest. So if the largest value appears several times, the answer is that same value.</p>\n","input":"<ul>\n<li>The first line contains the integer <code>N</code>.</li>\n<li>Each of the next <code>N</code> lines contains an integer.</li>\n</ul>\n","output":"<p>A single integer: the second largest value.</p>\n","constraints":"<ul>\n<li>2 ≤ N ≤ 1 000</li>\n<li>−1 000 000 000 ≤ each value ≤ 1 000 000 000</li>\n</ul>\n","hints":["<p>Keep two variables: <code>first</code>, the largest value seen so far, and <code>second</code>, the second largest.</p>\n","<p>To start, read the first two values, store the larger one in <code>first</code> and the other one in <code>second</code>.</p>\n","<p>A new value <code>x</code> can beat <code>first</code> (and then the old <code>first</code> becomes <code>second</code>), or only <code>second</code>, or nothing at all.</p>\n"],"solution":"<p>Keep the two largest values seen so far up to date, with <code>first ≥ second</code>. At the start, read two values and put them in the right order. Then, for each new value <code>x</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>if <code>x &gt; first</code>, the old first moves to second place: <code>second = first</code>, then <code>first = x</code>;</li>\n<li>otherwise, if <code>x &gt; second</code>, <code>x</code> only takes second place: <code>second = x</code>;</li>\n<li>otherwise, nothing changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The order of the two assignments in the first case matters: if you write <code>first = x</code> first, the old value of <code>first</code> is lost.</p>\n<p>This reasoning also handles repeated values. With <code>9, 4, 9</code>, the third <code>9</code> is not larger than <code>first</code>, but it is larger than <code>second</code> (4): it takes second place, and the answer is <code>9</code>, as required. Also be careful not to start from <code>0</code>: all the values can be negative.</p>\n"}},"solutionCode":{"python":"n = int(input())\na = int(input())\nb = int(input())\n# premier >= second : les deux plus grandes valeurs vues jusqu'ici.\nif a >= b:\n    premier = a\n    second = b\nelse:\n    premier = b\n    second = a\nfor i in range(n - 2):\n    x = int(input())\n    if x > premier:\n        second = premier\n        premier = x\n    elif x > second:\n        second = x\nprint(second)\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    int n, a, b;\n    cin >> n >> a >> b;\n    // premier >= second : les deux plus grandes valeurs vues jusqu'ici.\n    int premier, second;\n    if (a >= b) {\n        premier = a;\n        second = b;\n    } else {\n        premier = b;\n        second = a;\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {\n        int x;\n        cin >> x;\n        if (x > premier) {\n            second = premier;\n            premier = x;\n        } else if (x > second) {\n            second = x;\n        }\n    }\n    cout << second << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}\n"},"starter":{"fr":{"python":null,"cpp":null},"en":{"python":null,"cpp":null}},"samples":[{"input":"5\n12\n5\n30\n18\n7\n","output":"18\n"},{"input":"3\n9\n4\n9\n","output":"9\n"},{"input":"2\n-3\n-8\n","output":"-8\n"}],"tests":[{"input":"2\n4\n9\n","hash":"9fb1517a89c1e8508408cff9eca7b0d59abec9e13257f363d852720111f35eeb"},{"input":"2\n9\n4\n","hash":"79ea1fbca07650d0f77f5e0d050dd920d18914f2e15bcdee19209c8686751c92"},{"input":"4\n5\n5\n5\n5\n","hash":"cb7e78e60fe3939aeed1996abed5d930009f43c5349b0607d9a6803a8195b788"},{"input":"6\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n6\n","hash":"caf5b842e34b9c210423ad316059ff4935821c44afe32823069cc0cc4a4b50a3"},{"input":"6\n6\n5\n4\n3\n2\n1\n","hash":"19d09091ce6c590eba7905e4ec4c6f3faa2b1bda8423315b1ccd414f0b03ce05"},{"input":"3\n1000000000\n-1000000000\n1000000000\n","hash":"f9849d1215df92ef7d898ee43a2b8a89d1e7de277615c363d98ec011998d0a4e"},{"input":"1000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000\n-1000000000